## 4. Data contract (project format v1) — the core deliverable

Stored as a directory `project.rim/` with `project.json` (manifest), `annotations/` (append-only segments), `thumbnails/`, `exports/`, `logs/`. Everything human-readable JSON (UTF-8), except optional mask arrays stored as chunked binary per the Zarr chunk scheme [S8, Inference].

### 4.1 Coordinate spaces (explicit, pinned, tested)
1. **Data space (voxel indices)**: integer tuples in **stored array axis order**. The manifest records `axes: [{name, kind, unit?, size}, …]` in array order (e.g. `t,z,c,y,x`); the order is *data*, never implied.
2. **World space (physical)**: float64, per-axis physical units (`µm`, `mm`, `s`); mapping `world_i = origin_i + scale_i × data_i` per axis (scale may be negative to represent a flipped acquisition axis — see §3). A later-optional 3×3 direction matrix (orientation) is reserved but **not** in v1; if an instrument reports orientation we keep it verbatim in provenance and display it as an explicit, non-applied note (M boundary).
3. **Canvas space (screen)**: derived at render time from world space plus view zoom/pan; never persisted except as ephemeral session state.

Rules: annotation geometry is stored in **data space** (integer-safe for voxel-anchored items; float64 where sub-voxel precision is needed); export can re-express it in world or pixel space, and every export file states which space it uses — following QuPath's precedent of printing the units-and-origin contract in the export docs [S6, adapted as Choice]. Precision: JSON numbers are float64; float32 is forbidden for stored coordinates and transforms (drift check [W5-proposed]). The signed-scale/positive-size rule of §3 is normative, with the W1c arithmetic as its pinned test.

### 4.2 Images (project entries)
Each entry mirrors QuPath's proven shape [S5, Inference]: `entry_id` (UUIDv4, immutable), `name`, `source` {absolute path, **sha256**, size bytes, importer id, import timestamp}, `axes` (from §4.1), `raw_metadata` (**verbatim** captured metadata blob, retained even when we normalize), `calibration_status` (`ok | partial | unknown | user_corrected` with the user's correction recorded as an operation, never overwritten silently), and `warnings[]` from import (e.g. "ImageJ hyperstack: 6-D data; axes assumed TZYX from ImageDescription; verify"). Source images are opened read-only; **in-place modification of sources is out of scope** (brief).

### 4.3 Annotations
`{annotation_id (UUID), entry_id, kind: point|polygon|mask, geometry (data space), attachment: {plane: {z,t} } | volume | whole_acquisition, style_id, created/modified, author, note}`. Slice/time-bound vs volume/whole-acquisition attachment is therefore explicit, and a UI toggle shows out-of-plane annotations ghosted. Styles are named reusable objects (`{stroke, fill, vertex_size (world-derived via |scale|), label}`) stored at project level [Choice, informed by S6 classifications-in-properties].

### 4.4 Display state vs data (never the twain)
Per-entry view state (visible channels, per-channel LUT, window/level = `contrast_limits`, active slice/time) lives in `session_state` — saved for convenience, clearly separated, **never written into sample data** [Fact-anchored design: napari's contrast/limits are layer properties, not data mutations; S1–S4 context]. A geometric transform or resampling is a **recorded operation**: `{op_id, kind: rotate|resample|crop, input_ref, params, output_ref (derived image entry), timestamp, invoked_by}` appended to the operations journal. Derived entries carry `derived_from[]` so the UI can badge them, satisfying "visible distinction between original data and derived views" and enabling "verify what transformed and what remained unchanged": the export manifest lists, per item, its source sha256 and operation chain.

### 4.5 Undo/redo and the journal
Every annotation edit is a command `{before, after, inverse}` on a per-entry undo stack (deep-copied or inverse-applicable deltas) [Choice: command pattern, standard; kept out of sources]. Long operations (import, resample, export) get progress + cooperative cancellation tokens; cancellation leaves the last committed state intact (§6).

