## 6. Minimum workflow, mapped

| Brief step | Mechanism |
|---|---|
| Create project | `project.rim/` scaffold + `project.json` v1 manifest |
| Import without rewriting originals | open read-only; record sha256+size+mtime; stage nothing unless a derived copy is requested; import finishes by committing the manifest entry (atomic) |
| Inspect metadata & provenance | metadata panel renders `raw_metadata` verbatim + our normalized interpretation side by side; any mismatch is a visible warning |
| Navigate & adjust display | channel toggles, window/level, slice/time steppers → session state only |
| Annotate | points/polygons/masks with attachment plane/volume/whole-acquisition; styles; undo/redo; keyboard-first tools |
| Save | atomic manifest replace + append annotation segment (§6.1) |
| Close / reopen on another machine | hash-based relocation dialog (§6.2) |
| Export table/overlay + small derived image | GeoJSON or CSV (table), PNG overlay (small, rendered), each with an export manifest stating space/units/origin and source sha256 [S6-informed] |
| Verify what transformed | operations journal + export manifest diffs (source sha unchanged; chain of recorded ops listed) |

### 6.1 Atomic save and recovery
**[Inference from S5 (summary.json written together with data.qpdata) + standard POSIX semantics]** Writes are: annotation segment `seg_N.json` → fsync → manifest swap `project.json.tmp` → fsync → `os.replace` onto `project.json`. An interrupted save leaves the previous manifest valid and at most one orphan `.tmp`/segment, which the next open cleans or adopts. Partial imports: entry is written under `staging/` and only becomes visible when committed to the manifest. Failed exports write to `exports/…partial` and are never referenced by the manifest. Recovery = always load last valid manifest; segments beyond it are adoptable. Validation harness [W4-proposed].

### 6.2 Identity, relocation, bundles vs references
Source identity = **content sha256** (not path). On reopen, entries are resolved by path-then-hash; a mismatch opens a relocation dialog listing unresolved sources with their recorded absolute paths (no silent rewriting) — a deliberate strengthening of QuPath's documented per-machine duplicate-manifest pattern [S5]. Bundle modes [Choice]: **(i) reference** (default): manifest points at external files anywhere on the shared drive — cheap, but moving sources requires relocation; **(ii) collected bundle**: project + small sources (< configurable cap, default 256 MiB) copied into `project.rim/sources/`; large sources stay referenced with a printed warning that the bundle is not self-contained. Trade-off is stated in the UI at bundle-creation time: portability vs duplication of multi-GB data. This mirrors the docum­ented QuPath behavior of projects whose image paths are per-machine while data sidecars travel with the project [S5].

