## 7. Support boundary and failing safely

**v1 supported [Choice bounded by S7]:** classic TIFF (≤32-bit int, 16/32/64 float), BigTIFF, tiled & stripped, ImageJ hyperstack, OME-TIFF read, PNG. Multi-file OME-TIFF datasets only if all siblings are present at import. Everything else (`.czi`, `.nd2`, `.lif`, DICOM series, …) is **declared unsupported in v1** and listed in the UI as such. Rationale: a 6-person team can test exactly this matrix against fixture files; tifffile itself draws its boundary comparably and still documents instrument-metadata pathologies (MMStack "often corrupted or wrong") [S7], which is the realistic scale of ambition for correct broad-format support.

**Failing without destroying the project:** an unsupported or ambiguous file is *never partially imported*. Ambiguous-but-parseable cases (missing units, axis order inferred from heuristic, negative/absent calibration) open the **import review dialog**: raw metadata verbatim + our interpretation + explicit, per-field correction controls; accepting records `user_corrected` with the correction as a first-class operation; the original values stay in `raw_metadata` [brief requirement; Choice on dialog details]. If a previously-fine source disappears or its hash changes, the project still opens: the entry is flagged `unresolved`, annotations are retained and visibly quarantined until the source is re-linked by hash. A wrong format choice therefore costs a dialog, never data. This follows the QuPath refusal precedent: no public schema → do not guess, tell the user [S6].

